$ node -e "..." (jest cannot execute in this sandbox — no node_modules, see
above). Every new/changed test file was instead traced by hand against the
exact fake-Supabase chain semantics already established in
admin-partner-health.test.ts/ingestion.test.ts's style:
  - test/partner-health/org-access.test.ts: pure-function tests need no
    fake-chain tracing (resolveOrgHealthAccess's own fake only implements
    eq/in/order/limit/select/then, matching org-access.ts's calls, which
    never use .maybeSingle()/.single()).
  - test/admin-partner-health.test.ts: added `in` to the existing chain's
    passthrough-method list (org-access.ts's partner_registry query uses
    .in()); traced every new org-scoped test's table-handler shape against
    admin-partner-health.ts's actual query chain (select/eq/order/limit vs
    maybeSingle) to confirm each handler returns the shape the real code
    path expects at that exact call site.
  - test/partner-orgs.test.ts: extended the fake chain to tag each
    handler invocation with `terminal: 'maybeSingle'|'single'|'then'`
    (needed because requireOrgAdmin()'s own admin check and the members-list
    handler both query partner_organization_members with op='select' but
    different terminal methods, and returning an array where a single
    object is expected — or vice versa — would silently break the request
    without a type error to catch it, since this is untyped JS-shaped mock
    data). The "200 for exafy_admin" test was fixed mid-authoring: it
    originally awaited the request BEFORE setting up the table handler,
    which would have thrown "Unexpected table in test" had exafy_admin's
    short-circuit not skipped the query entirely — reordered so the handler
    is registered before the request fires, matching every other test in
    the file.

